{"name":"Bayberry Wax","hex":"#b6aa89","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bayberry wax","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An aromatic green vegetable wax from the surface of the fruit of the bayberry, used in the manufacture of scented products such as candles."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bayberry_wax"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bayberry wax","blocks":[{"text":"Bayberry wax is an aromatic green vegetable wax. It is removed from the surface of the fruit of the bayberry (wax-myrtle) shrub by boiling the fruits in water and skimming the wax from the surface of the water. Unlike other plant waxes, it is made up primarily of esters of lauric, myristic, and palmitic acid."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bayberry_wax"},{"title":"Myrica","blocks":[{"text":"Myrica is a genus of about 35–50 species of small trees and shrubs in the family Myricaceae, order Fagales. The genus has a wide distribution, including Africa, Asia, Europe, North America, and South America, and missing only from Antarctica and Oceania. Some botanists split the genus into two genera on the basis of the catkin and fruit structure, restricting Myrica to a few species, and treating the others in Morella."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Gagelstrauch%28Myrica_gale%29female.JPG/330px-Gagelstrauch%28Myrica_gale%29female.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Myrica"},{"title":"Wax","blocks":[{"text":"Waxes are a diverse class of organic compounds that are lipophilic solids that are malleable near ambient temperatures. They include higher alkanes and lipids, typically with melting points above about 40 °C (104 °F), melting to give low viscosity liquids. Waxes are insoluble in water but soluble in nonpolar organic solvents such as hexane, benzene and chloroform. Natural waxes of various types are produced by plants and animals and occur in petroleum."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Beeswax_foundation.jpg/330px-Beeswax_foundation.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wax"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Myrica_faya_fruit.jpg/250px-Myrica_faya_fruit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/01/Myrica_faya_fruit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Myrica_faya_fruit.jpg/250px-Myrica_faya_fruit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Myrica faya fruit","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMyrica_faya_fruit.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/MODOCeroline.jpg/500px-MODOCeroline.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/MODOCeroline.jpg/1280px-MODOCeroline.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/MODOCeroline.jpg/500px-MODOCeroline.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ceroline brand wax for floors and furniture, first half of 20th century. From the Museo del Objeto del Objeto collection.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMODOCeroline.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Montana_10_bg_061905.jpg/500px-Montana_10_bg_061905.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Montana_10_bg_061905.jpg/500px-Montana_10_bg_061905.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wax 